fuse: Kempston joystick, and accept plain RETRO_DEVICE_JOYPAD

The joystick did nothing in ZX Spectrum games despite fuse.cfg already
requesting Kempston (input_libretro_device_p1 = 513). The config was
never the problem.

RetroArch calls retro_set_controller_port_device(0, RETRO_DEVICE_JOYPAD)
after core init. Plain JOYPAD (value 1) matches none of the Spectrum
joystick cases in the switch, so it falls through to default:, which
records input_devices[port] but never assigns joystick_1_output. The
joystick is then silently dead whatever the user presses, and whatever
the frontend config asked for.

Two changes:
  1. port 0 defaults to Kempston rather than Cursor. Cursor maps the
     stick to keyboard keys; Kempston is what Spectrum software expects.
  2. plain RETRO_DEVICE_JOYPAD is treated as Kempston, so joystick input
     survives whatever the frontend sends.

Ported from the LyraZeroW project, which hit and solved this on the same
fuse commit (69a44421). Its log also notes the override directory is
case-sensitive: the core reports library_name "fuse", so the config
must live at config/fuse/fuse.cfg, not config/Fuse/Fuse.cfg.

Signed-off-by: RetroBPI_M2M

--- a/src/libretro.c
+++ b/src/libretro.c
@@ -539,7 +539,9 @@
    active_cheats = NULL;
 
    // Set default controllers
-   retro_set_controller_port_device( 0, RETRO_DEVICE_CURSOR_JOYSTICK );
+   /* RetroBPI: Kempston, not Cursor. Kempston is what Spectrum games
+    * overwhelmingly expect; Cursor maps the stick to keyboard keys. */
+   retro_set_controller_port_device( 0, RETRO_DEVICE_KEMPSTON_JOYSTICK );
    retro_set_controller_port_device( 1, RETRO_DEVICE_KEMPSTON_JOYSTICK );
    retro_set_controller_port_device( 2, RETRO_DEVICE_SPECTRUM_KEYBOARD );
 }
@@ -975,6 +977,22 @@
 void retro_set_controller_port_device(unsigned port, unsigned device)
 {
    log_cb(RETRO_LOG_INFO, "port %u device %08x\n", port, device);
+   /* RetroBPI: treat plain RETRO_DEVICE_JOYPAD as Kempston.
+    *
+    * RetroArch calls this with RETRO_DEVICE_JOYPAD (value 1) after core init,
+    * overriding whatever default was set at startup. Plain JOYPAD matches none
+    * of the Spectrum joystick cases below, so it falls through to default:,
+    * which records input_devices[port] but never sets joystick_1_output -- the
+    * joystick is then silently dead no matter what the user presses.
+    *
+    * Mapping it to Kempston keeps joystick input alive whatever the frontend
+    * sends, and Kempston is the right default for Spectrum software.
+    *
+    * Ported from the LyraZeroW project, which hit and solved this on the same
+    * fuse commit (69a44421). */
+   if (device == RETRO_DEVICE_JOYPAD)
+      device = RETRO_DEVICE_KEMPSTON_JOYSTICK;
+
